---
import { Anchor, Code, CodeBlock, Container, Heading, Stack, Text } from "@ps1ui/core";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { CONTAINER_PX, INSTALL_COMMAND } from "../lib/site";
import { href } from "../lib/url";

const styles = `import "@ps1ui/core/styles.css";`;

const usage = `import "@ps1ui/core/styles.css";
import { PS1Root, Button, Heading } from "@ps1ui/core";

export function App() {
  return (
    <PS1Root>
      <Heading level={1}>Hello, PS1 UI</Heading>
      <Button variant="primary">Click me</Button>
    </PS1Root>
  );
}`;

const wholePage = `<html data-ps1ui-theme="system">`;

const nested = `<PS1Root theme="light">
  {/* everything in here renders with the light palette */}
  <PS1Root theme="dark">
    {/* ...except this subtree, back to dark */}
  </PS1Root>
</PS1Root>`;

const cssTarget = `build: { cssTarget: ["chrome123", "firefox120", "safari17.5"] }`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="Install @ps1ui/core and render your first components."
>
  <Container size="md" px={CONTAINER_PX}>
    <Stack direction="column" gap="2xl">
      <Heading level={1} size="2xl">Getting Started</Heading>

      <Stack direction="column" gap="md">
        <Heading level={2} size="lg">Install</Heading>
        <Text>
          React 19 is a peer dependency — install it alongside the package.
        </Text>
        <CodeBlock language="bash" code={INSTALL_COMMAND} />
      </Stack>

      <Stack direction="column" gap="md">
        <Heading level={2} size="lg">Import the stylesheet</Heading>
        <Text>
          The JS entry ships no styles. Import the CSS once at your app's entry
          point — it brings the design tokens, the canvas (dark by default),
          and every component's styles.
        </Text>
        <CodeBlock language="tsx" code={styles} />
      </Stack>

      <Stack direction="column" gap="md">
        <Heading level={2} size="lg">Wrap your app in <Code>PS1Root</Code></Heading>
        <Text>
          <Code>PS1Root</Code> establishes the container-query context that responsive props
          resolve against. Without it, responsive props silently fall back to their <Code
            >base</Code
          > values. Wrap once, at the top of your tree. Layout primitives can each become a
          nested context of their own via <Code>queryContainer</Code>, but never do so by
          default.
        </Text>
        <CodeBlock language="tsx" code={usage} />
      </Stack>

      <Stack direction="column" gap="md">
        <Heading level={2} size="lg">Theming</Heading>
        <Text>
          Dark is the default. Put <Code>data-ps1ui-theme="light"</Code> on <Code
            >&lt;html&gt;</Code
          > to theme the whole page, or <Code>"system"</Code> to follow the OS instead:
        </Text>
        <CodeBlock language="html" code={wholePage} />
        <Text>
          To theme just part of a page, wrap that subtree in <Code
            >&lt;PS1Root theme="light"&gt;</Code
          > instead — a themed <Code>PS1Root</Code> paints its own canvas and text color too, so the
          subtree is self-contained, and nesting <Code>PS1Root</Code>s with different themes works
          as expected:
        </Text>
        <CodeBlock language="tsx" code={nested} />
        <Text>
          If your bundler minifies CSS with Lightning CSS (Vite's default), check its CSS target.
          Below Chrome 123 / Firefox 120 / Safari 17.5, Lightning CSS downlevels <Code
            >light-dark()</Code
          > into a <Code>prefers-color-scheme</Code>-only fallback, and a per-subtree <Code
            >PS1Root theme</Code
          > stops switching — <Code>&lt;html&gt;</Code>-level theming and <Code>"system"</Code> keep
          working either way. Raise the target to fix it:
        </Text>
        <CodeBlock language="ts" code={cssTarget} />
      </Stack>

      <Stack direction="column" gap="md">
        <Heading level={2} size="lg">Next</Heading>
        <Text>
          Browse the <Anchor href={href("components/")}>components</Anchor> to
          see everything in the box.
        </Text>
      </Stack>
    </Stack>
  </Container>
</BaseLayout>
